                <div class="project-content-text">
                    <p class="project-intro">A cross-country Bayesian VAR and time-varying BVAR analysis testing how energy production mixes shape macroeconomic outcomes in France, Italy and Spain.</p>
                    
                    <h2>Project Overview</h2>
                    <p>This study tests four competing hypotheses from the energy-growth literature (Conservation, Growth, Neutrality and Feedback) using a 40-variable monthly panel spanning France, Italy and Spain between 2007 and 2024. By separating the analysis into a production-side and a consumption-side transmission channel, the paper evaluates how each country's energy mix interacts with industrial output and household consumption across the sovereign debt crisis, the Covid-19 pandemic and the 2022 energy crisis.</p>

                    <h2>Technical Approach</h2>
                    <p>The core estimation relies on a fixed-coefficient Bayesian VAR with a Minnesota Prior, estimated via Gibbs sampling, producing Impulse Response Functions and Forecast Error Variance Decompositions across four Cholesky-ordered transmission channels. A Time-Varying Parameter BVAR (Primiceri, 2005) extends the analysis to capture how these dynamics evolve across five distinct macroeconomic regimes.</p>
                    
                    <p class="project-tools"><strong>Tools:</strong> R, Bayesian VAR (Minnesota Prior), Time-Varying Parameter BVAR (Primiceri 2005), Gibbs Sampling, Kalman Smoother, X-13-ARIMA Seasonal Adjustment.</p>
                </div>

